fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1099", description: some(value: "A POSITIONAL `ListLiteral(...)` WRITTEN BY NAME IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM `[...]`, so the reflect entity has become unwritable in a term position — and its reify twin is unmatchable.\n\nMECHANISM, read not measured beyond the grep below. The parser lowers `[a, b]` to `Fn{ListLiteral, pos_args: [a, b]}` (parse/convert.rs, `BuildFrame::CollectionLiteral`) and lowers a written `ListLiteral(a, b)` to the SAME shape once `remap_symbol` resolves the name. After WI-1096 the loader lowers that shape to the `cons`/`nil` spine wherever no declared type names another collection, so the only spelling of the reflect entity that survives a term position is the NAMED-arg one, `ListLiteral(elements: ...)` -- which is not the shape anything builds.\n\nWHAT THAT COSTS, two things:\n - `node_occurrence.rs` (`occurrence_to_term`, WI-027) builds a list-literal occurrence's TERM TWIN as POSITIONAL `ListLiteral(e...)`. A meta-rule in the style of typing_pass_spec.anthill's `:- occurrence_term(?e, var_ref(name: ?n))` therefore cannot be written for a list literal: the pattern the author would write lowers to a cons spine and never matches the twin.\n - Round-trip. `TermPrinter` renders a flat `ListLiteral` and a ground `cons` spine IDENTICALLY as `[1, 2]`, so a reflect-built `ListLiteral` persisted to disk in an undeclared position reloads as a cons spine. The in-memory twin and its on-disk form are different terms.\n\nZERO CORPUS USERS TODAY, which is why this is filed rather than fixed inline: grep over stdlib / examples / anthill-todo / rustland finds only `case ListLiteral(elements: elems)` in an operation body (docs/proposals/typing_pass_spec.anthill:542), which is expression world and unaffected. So this is a latent expressiveness hole, not a live break.\n\nTHE FIX HAS A PRECEDENT AND IS SMALL, but the DECISION is a surface-semantics one and is why this is a ticket. WI-927 hit the identical shape -- `Box[T = Int64]` and `Box(value: 1)` lower to one `Term::Fn` with one functor, and only the SURFACE tells them apart -- and solved it with a parse-level marker (`SimpleTermStore::mark_type_application` / `is_type_application`, ir.rs, beside `mark_projection`). The same mechanism applies: mark the bracket surface at `BuildFrame::CollectionLiteral`, and have `list_literal_lowering` (kb/load.rs) lower ONLY a marked term, so a by-name `ListLiteral(...)` stays the reflect entity.\n\nWHAT MUST BE DECIDED FIRST, and it is the reason not to just do it: whether a written `ListLiteral(a, b)` SHOULD mean the reflect entity rather than a list. The alternative reading is that the name is simply how the literal spells itself and both are lists, in which case the twin should be built some other way and the round-trip fixed at the printer instead. WI-927 answered the analogous question one way; this ticket must answer it explicitly rather than inherit it.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n - a positional `ListLiteral(?e)` written BY NAME in a rule body / fact head does NOT lower, driven by a test that fails before the fix, with the `[?e]` bracket twin kept beside it as the control that still DOES lower (so the fix is attributable to the surface and not to the spelling of the functor);\n - a meta-rule matching an occurrence's list-literal term twin ANSWERS -- the case that motivates this, driven end to end through `occurrence_term`, not asserted structurally;\n - the ROUND-TRIP row: a reflect-built `ListLiteral` in an undeclared position printed and reloaded is still a `ListLiteral`. If that requires a printer change too, say so and make it, or state why the two forms may share a printed form;\n - the named-arg spelling `ListLiteral(elements: ...)` keeps working (WI-1096's guard) -- it is a THIRD shape and must not be collapsed into either answer;\n - say at the test site which tests fail when the change is backed out and which pass either way by design;\n - full workspace green via scripts/test.sh (a parse + loader change).\n\nSOURCE: found by the second `/code-review` pass on WI-1096's commit (8f188953), which measured the query-converter desync in the same sweep. That half was a live regression and was fixed inline; this half is latent and is the part that needs a decision."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-15T05:50:40Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1099", author: "claude", content: "THE \"ZERO CORPUS USERS, SO IT IS LATENT\" CLAIM IN THIS DESCRIPTION IS FALSE, and it was falsified within minutes of filing. The mechanism half stands; the severity half was wrong, and so was the reason for filing rather than fixing.\n\nWHAT REFUTED IT. `wi040_reserved_vocab_test::query_pattern_bare_list_literal_resolves_qualified` writes the pattern `ListLiteral(?x)` and asserts it resolves to `anthill.reflect.ListLiteral`. It went RED as soon as the query converter started lowering -- the pattern had become a `cons` spine, so the functor was `cons`. That is a LIVE, deliberately pinned user of the by-name positional spelling, and it is exactly the case this ticket described as having none.\n\nWHY MY GREP MISSED IT: I grepped the `.anthill` corpus (stdlib, examples, anthill-todo, rustland's own anthill files). This user is a Rust test with the pattern as an inline string. A \"who uses this spelling\" census over a language whose tooling is written in another language has TWO corpora, and I searched one. Same lesson as WI-1034 (\"corpus is not the fixtures\") in a new dress.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE FILING DECISION. The ticket said the fix was deferred because \"whether a written `ListLiteral(a, b)` should mean the reflect entity\" is a surface-semantics decision needing an owner. That decision was already MADE and already pinned -- wi040 is the record of it. There was nothing to decide, so the mechanism half was implemented inline with the WI-1096 follow-up rather than left open:\n\n - `SimpleTermStore::collection_literals` + `mark_collection_literal` / `is_collection_literal` (parse/ir.rs), beside `type_applications` and for the identical reason (WI-927: `Box[T = Int64]` vs `Box(value: 1)` build one term and only the surface separates them);\n - marked at `BuildFrame::CollectionLiteral` (parse/convert.rs), the sole producer of the bracket surface;\n - read by `kb::load::list_literal_lowering`, the ONE decision function both the loader and the query converter call, so the surface test cannot be applied by one and not the other.\n\nDRIVEN, loader side: `wi1096_list_literal_lowering_test::only_the_bracket_surface_is_the_list_literal` -- `[1, 2]` destructures as a cons spine, `ListLiteral(1, 2)` written by name does NOT, and is still matchable AS the entity by the same spelling. wi040 is the sibling driver on the query side and needed no change once the mark was in.\n\nWHAT REMAINS, and this ticket keeps it -- both are about the OCCURRENCE twin and neither is answered by the mark:\n\n 1. `occurrence_to_term` (node_occurrence.rs, WI-027) builds a list-literal occurrence's term twin as POSITIONAL `ListLiteral(e...)`. With the mark in place a source-written `ListLiteral(?e)` now stays that shape, so the twin is matchable in principle -- but that must be DRIVEN end to end through `occurrence_term`, in the style of typing_pass_spec.anthill's `:- occurrence_term(?e, var_ref(name: ?n))`, before it is claimed. Reading the shapes and calling them equal is what this ticket already got wrong once.\n\n 2. THE ROUND-TRIP IS STILL BROKEN AND THE MARK CANNOT FIX IT. `TermPrinter` renders a flat `ListLiteral` and a ground `cons` spine IDENTICALLY as `[1, 2]`. So a reflect-built `ListLiteral` in an undeclared position, printed to disk, reloads through the bracket surface -- marked -- and comes back a cons spine. The mark distinguishes what the AUTHOR wrote; it cannot distinguish what the PRINTER wrote, because the printer threw the distinction away. That is a printer question (does the reflect entity need a distinct printed form, or does the twin need to stop being positional?), and it is the part of this ticket that still needs a decision.\n\nSo: acceptance rows 1 and 4 of the original description are delivered; rows 2 and 3 are what is left, and row 3 (the round trip) is the one carrying a real design question.\n\nSOURCE: refuted by running the suite after implementing the WI-1096 query-converter fix.", at: "2026-08-14T14:50:22Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1099", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED, and BOTH remaining rows had a wrong premise in this ticket. Row 2's mechanism was misfiled; row 3's two candidate remedies are both refuted, and the fix is a third thing.\n\nROW 2 -- THE TWIN WAS UNREACHABLE, AND THE MARK HAD NOTHING TO DO WITH IT. The ticket said the pattern an author writes, `ListLiteral(?a, ?b)`, lowered to a cons spine and so could never match the positional twin; with the mark in, it reasoned, the twin \"is matchable in principle\". MEASURED with the mark in and before any change here: it matched NOTHING -- and neither did `occurrence_term(?e, cons(head: ?h, tail: ?t))` over an occurrence spelled `cons(head: 1, tail: nil())`, which contains no list literal at all. ONE NAME, TWO READERS: the goal form `occurrence_term(?e, pattern)` does not call `occurrence_to_term` (the value-domain `occurrence_term(occ) -> Term` does); it matches through the `ReflectedExpr` lens, and that lens reflected LITERAL LEAVES ONLY -- every compound `Expr` read `Opaque`, matching nothing. So the meta-rule style the ticket cites from typing_pass_spec.anthill could not be written for a list literal, a `var_ref`, or anything but a literal. I measured the shape of one reader and filed against the other.\n\nTHE FIX IS UNIVERSAL, NOT A LIST-LITERAL ARM: the lens now delegates to the plain occurrence view for every form but `Expr::Const`, whose `int_lit(value: ...)` reading is the one legitimate divergence. The plain view already IS the `occurrence_to_term` twin, arm for arm and by explicit design (WI-814 wrapped forms, WI-683/WI-1014 collection literals, WI-520 applications/constructors), so `Opaque` there was the WI-425 cross-carrier miss in its plainest form -- a pattern the reifier WOULD build for an occurrence could not be matched against that occurrence. Note this REMOVES a second reading of `Expr` rather than adding one, which is the direction WI-246's feedback asked for.\n\nROW 3 -- THE PRINTER CANNOT BE THE PLACE, AND BOTH OFFERED REMEDIES ARE REFUSED.\n - \"give the reflect entity a distinct printed form\": there is no bit to read. The KB is hash-consed, so a `[1, 2]` the loader DECLINED to lower (a `Set[T = Int64]` field, spec 4.6) and a written `ListLiteral(1, 2)` are the SAME TermId. The parse-time mark cannot be carried in -- it survives only because parse terms are not deduped (the same reason `mark_type_application` lives there, WI-927).\n - \"make the twin stop being positional\": the twin must be what the parser builds for `[...]`, which is positional. Named args would reopen the disagreement WI-1014 closed for TupleLiteral.\nSo the two forms DO share `TermPrinter`'s form, and the ticket's alternative (\"state why\") is the right answer for the printer. The round trip is fixed one level out, at the CALLER, because two callers were asking different questions of one function: `print_fact` needs text that RELOADS to this term (so a flat ListLiteral is written BY NAME, the one spelling no position lowers); the retract content key needs a print INVARIANT under the loader's lowering (`apply_retracts` compares a print of the on-disk PARSE term against a print of the LOADED head, and 4.6 lowers between them). `TermPrinter::reload_faithful` vs the default.\n\nMEASURED. Before: `fact stored(ListLiteral(1, 2))` loads as ListLiteral/2p0n and `fact spine([1, 2])` as cons/0p2n, and BOTH print `[1, 2]`; reloaded, the flat one comes back a spine. After: persisted as `ListLiteral(1, 2)` / `[1, 2]` / `ListLiteral()` and each reloads as itself, while the content key still reads `stored([1, 2])` on both sides. Back-outs: drop the lens delegation and 4 rows fail; drop the persist print and 3 fail; give the CONTENT KEY the faithful mode too and exactly 1 fails -- `a_persisted_literal_is_still_retractable`, leaving the fact on disk silently. That last is the only row measuring the split.\n\nREVIEW PASS (/code-review high), 4 findings, all dispositioned by MEASUREMENT:\n - \"the persist print makes on-disk text injective while the key stays collapsed, so retracting one of two same-functor facts erases the other's line\": REAL, and measured IDENTICAL with the change and without it (without it the pair persists as two identical lines and one retract still erases both). The loss is in the KEY, not the print; pre-existing, documented at the site, and the path without it is IndexedFileStore's SPAN retract (WI-187). Not fixable at the printer -- see the next item.\n - \"the `*_lit` wrap is root-only, so a nested `int_lit(value: ?)` pattern matches nothing\": TRUE, not a regression (before, the whole pattern matched nothing), and it is the shipped idiom -- descend with `sub_occurrences`, reflect each child with its own `occurrence_term`. Now DRIVEN by a row rather than left as prose, and the lens doc no longer reads as if the wrap were recursive.\n - \"`as_bind_value` is the one occurrence read not routed through the shared helpers\": TRUE and deliberate -- the builtin's contract is that `occurrence_term(?e, ?t)` binds the OCCURRENCE, identity preserved. Stated at the site; the WI-425 invariant block updated to name the fourth reader.\n - \"match the QUALIFIED name like the loader does\": REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT. This printer also runs over a `ParsedFile`, whose `TermSource::qualified_name` is the trait DEFAULT (the short name), so keying on the qualified name splits the two sides of the retract comparison: `a_persisted_literal_is_still_retractable` fails and the fact stays on disk -- while the ENTIRE anthill-todo suite (137) stays green, because it retracts by SPAN and never reaches this key. The finding's observation is real (a user's own `entity ListLiteral` prints as `[...]`); the remedy named is not available here, and the reason is now written at the test site.\n\nNOT CLAIMED, driven as its own row: the goal form matches the TERM TWIN, not the reflect-DATA vocabulary. `foo(?x)` answers over a `foo(1)` occurrence; `apply(fn: ?f, args: ?a)` and `constructor(name: ?c, args: ?a)` -- what typing_pass_spec.anthill's synth rules are written in -- do not. Reaching that vocabulary is a different change whose right shape is WI-246's (build rule-body atoms as occurrences natively in the front end). Also not claimed: a ground cons spine persisted into a rival-collection slot still reloads flat -- measured, pre-existing, and refused twice over (every persisted list would become a cons chain, and the parse side of one does not fold back to `[...]`).\n\nSpec 4.6 updated: the by-name spelling is the reflect entity POSITIONALLY too (the sentence still said \"with named arguments\", which the surface mark superseded), plus a Printed form paragraph. Full workspace green (29 binaries), scaland green (490); nothing to port -- scaland has no list-literal lowering and no printer.", at: "2026-08-15T05:50:28Z")
